sentiment analysis has 3 stages, Tokenizer-Model-Post Processing



Tokenizer turns everything into numbers
default of sentiment tracker is distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english
to download information from Model Hub, we use:

from transformers import AutoTokenizer

checkpoint = "distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english"    #not an actual command, ot's just a variable 
tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(checkpoint)

now you will get a list of dictionary that is ready to feed to the model, convert IDs into Tensors:

raw_inputs = [
    "I've been waiting for a HuggingFace course my whole life.",
    "I hate this so much!",
]
inputs = tokenizer(raw_inputs, padding=True, truncation=True, return_tensors="pt")   #return_tensor to specify the tensors we want back
print(inputs)





Download to Model:

from transformers import AutoModel

checkpoint = "distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english"
model = AutoModel.from_pretrained(checkpoint)

this will create a huge output, usually has 3Ds
Batch Size, Sequence Length, and Hidden Size      ex: torch.Size([2,16,768])

model has 1 or few hidden layers. the output is sent directly to the head to be processed.

There are many different architectures available in 🤗 Transformers, with each one designed around tackling a specific task.

*Model (retrieve the hidden states)
*ForCausalLM
*ForMaskedLM
*ForMultipleChoice
*ForQuestionAnswering
*ForSequenceClassification
*ForTokenClassification
and others 🤗


Using a model with a sequence classifier head:

from transformers import AutoModelForSequenceClassification
checkpoint = "distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english"
model = AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained(checkpoint)
outputs = model(**inputs)

# output command

print(outputs.logits.shape)




to softmax logits:

import torch

predictions = torch.nn.functional.softmax(outputs.logits, dim=-1)
print(predictions)



Creating a Transformer

from transformers import AutoModel           # fetch model architecture for a given checkpoint
model = AutoModel.from_pretrained("bert-base-cased")      # start by creating

model.save_pretrained("directory_on_my_computer")  # saving and loading

from transformers import AutoModel
model = AutoModel.from_pretrained("directory_on_my_computer")    # to reuse a saved model

model.push_to_hub("my-awesome-model")       # to share your model + the tokenization to the Hub
from transformers import AutoModel
model = AutoModel.from_pretrained("your-username/my-awesome-model")

from transformers import AutoTokenizer
tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("bert-base-cased")       #encoding text
encoded_input = tokenizer("Hello, I'm a single sentence!")
print(encoded_input)     # you get input_ids, token_type_ids, attention_mark 

tokenizer.decode(encoded_input["input_ids"])        # to decode and back to original text

encoded_input = tokenizer("How are you?", "I'm fine, thank you!")   # to encode multiple sentences at once
print(encoded_input)

encoded_input = tokenizer("How are you?", "I'm fine, thank you!", return_tensors="pt")   # to encode multiple and return tensors
print(encoded_input)

encoded_input = tokenizer(
    ["How are you?", "I'm fine, thank you!"],
    padding=True,      # padding makes the sentences the same size by adding "padding"
    truncation=True,   # if sequences longer than the mode can handle
    max_length=5,
    return_tensors="pt",
)
print(encoded_input)       # this all makes the tensors the exact same size




